assistant
There's the obvious concern for any system with lots of data which runs into memory constraints, say a distributed system storing things in files/partitions.

There's also a potential downside here of loading such data files at full extents vs being lazy about these chunks. Your distributed system could get really overwhelmed if a user starts loading several large arrays together, that would cause an impact for the whole system since all the storage locations were allocated within its total capacity, with possibly large loss in throughput, or latency issues in response.

Depending how often a client invokes such operations if memory usage issues appear often would suggest caching your arrays after they've been processed, perhaps with the entire processing operation cached, so retrieval is very fast once that is available. If these sorts operations do show up regularly a caching layer such as memcached/cache2 could easily handle fetching them off before making them available fully to processes that need their content.

Another common practice is chunked downloads with larger ones saved externally for offline reuse in case a sudden load up becomes an issue when those big arrays need re-sampling in, these sort of systems often also work in chunks when transferring them locally.

Also depending how much space your memory allocation system generally allows for free memory on behalf the OS's could do clever tricks to free memory that was currently dedicated somewhere else when retrieving these arrays to reuse that capacity, for instance freeing the system buffers here to use up, if there's some memory currently in process buffers reserved
